<p>An after builders clean is a specialist deep clean that removes the fine dust, debris, paint splashes, plaster marks and sticky residue left behind once building or renovation work is finished. It covers every surface from ceilings and light fittings down to skirting boards, inside cupboards and floors, so the space is ready to live in, let or hand over.</p>
<p>It goes well beyond a normal clean because construction dust is extremely fine and gets everywhere, including places you'd never usually think to clean. Our team carries out after builders cleans on extensions, loft conversions, new kitchens and full refurbishments across Hull and East Yorkshire, and this guide sets out exactly what's included and how to get the most from it.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>An after builders clean tackles fine dust, splashes and residue from top to bottom, inside and outside every fitting.</li><li>Book it after all trades have finished, before furniture goes back and before carpets are cleaned.</li><li>Fine dust keeps settling for days, so larger jobs often need a light follow-up clean.</li><li>Builders usually do a basic tidy-up; a professional clean is a separate, far more detailed job.</li></ul></div>
<h2>What is an after builders clean?</h2>
<p>Most builders leave a site "broom clean": rubble and offcuts gone, floors swept. That's very different from a home you can actually use. Plaster and wood dust settles on every ledge, works its way into cupboards and drawers, coats radiators and sticks to anything slightly greasy.</p>
<p>An after builders clean, sometimes called a post-construction or post-renovation clean, deals with all of that in a methodical, top-to-bottom order. It's usually a one-off job carried out by a small team using vacuums with fine-dust filters, plenty of microfibre cloths, scrapers and products that are safe on new surfaces. You can see how we approach it on our <a href="{{service:after-builders-cleaning}}">after builders cleaning service</a> page.</p>
<p>In the trade you'll also hear the "builders clean" and the "sparkle clean" described as separate stages. If you're comparing quotes, our guide to <a href="{{guide:builders-clean-vs-sparkle-clean}}">builders clean vs sparkle clean</a> explains the difference.</p>
<h2>What's included in an after builders clean: room by room</h2>
<h3>Throughout the property</h3>
<ul>
<li>Dusting ceilings, coving, corners and cobwebs</li>
<li>Cleaning light fittings and wiping the faceplates of switches and sockets</li>
<li>Wiping doors, frames, handles and hinges</li>
<li>Radiators, including between the fins where reachable, and exposed pipework</li>
<li>Skirting boards, window sills and architraves</li>
<li>Windows: inside glass, frames and tracks, plus removing stickers and protective film</li>
<li>Removing paint splashes, plaster spots and sealant smears from hard surfaces</li>
<li>Vacuuming and mopping all hard floors, with repeated passes to lift fine dust</li>
<li>Vacuuming carpets thoroughly</li>
</ul>
<h3>Kitchens</h3>
<ul>
<li>Inside and outside all cupboards and drawers</li>
<li>Worktops, splashbacks and sockets</li>
<li>Appliances inside and out, including peeling off protective film</li>
<li>Sink and taps, and removing silicone or adhesive residue</li>
<li>Extractor hood and filters</li>
</ul>
<p>New kitchens collect a surprising amount of dust inside the units. Our <a href="{{guide:kitchen-deep-clean-checklist}}">kitchen deep clean checklist</a> shows the level of detail involved.</p>
<h3>Bathrooms</h3>
<ul>
<li>Removing grout haze and tile adhesive from tiles</li>
<li>Cleaning toilets, basins, baths, taps and shower heads</li>
<li>Polishing shower screens and mirrors</li>
<li>Cleaning extractor fan covers</li>
</ul>
<h3>Bedrooms and living areas</h3>
<ul>
<li>Inside fitted wardrobes, shelving and alcove cupboards</li>
<li>Curtain poles, blind headrails and picture rails</li>
<li>Fireplaces and hearths, where dust and soot collect</li>
</ul>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Make sure your builder has finished all snagging, including touch-up painting and sealant, before the clean is booked. Every trade visit afterwards brings dust back in.</p></div>
<h2>What isn't usually included?</h2>
<p>An after builders clean is about cleaning, not waste removal or repairs. It usually doesn't include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Removing rubble, large offcuts, skips or bulk waste, which you should agree with your builder</li>
<li>Repairing damaged surfaces or touching up paintwork</li>
<li>Outside work such as patios, render or gutters, unless agreed in advance</li>
<li>Deep cleaning carpets, which is a separate job; many people add our <a href="{{service:carpet-upholstery-cleaning}}">carpet cleaning service</a> once the dust has settled</li>
<li>Paint or adhesive that has cured on a delicate finish and can't be removed without damaging it</li>
</ul>
<p>When you ask for a quote, check exactly what's in scope so there are no surprises. We agree a fixed quote, with a clear list of what's covered, before any work starts.</p>
<h2>When should you book an after builders clean?</h2>
<p>Timing matters more than most people expect. The best order is:</p>
<ol>
<li>All trades have finished, including electricians and decorators doing final fixes and touch-ups.</li>
<li>The builder has cleared waste, tools and dust sheets.</li>
<li>The after builders clean takes place.</li>
<li>Carpets are fitted or professionally cleaned.</li>
<li>Furniture, rugs and soft furnishings go back in.</li>
</ol>
<p>Fine dust keeps drifting down for a few days after the work stops. On bigger projects, a light follow-up clean a week or so later picks up what has settled. If you're still mid-project, our guide to <a href="{{guide:protect-home-during-renovation}}">protecting your home from dust during renovation</a> will reduce how much cleaning is needed at the end.</p>
<h2>How long does an after builders clean take?</h2>
<p>It depends on the size of the area, how much dust was created and how many splashes and residues there are. A single room after decorating can often be done by one cleaner in a few hours, while a whole-house refurbishment may need a team for a full day or more.</p>
<p>Photos help us give you a realistic estimate. Tell us which rooms were worked on, whether there was sanding or plastering, and whether flooring is new or existing.</p>
<h2>Can you do an after builders clean yourself?</h2>
<p>For a small decorating job, yes. Vacuum before you wipe, use damp rather than wet microfibre cloths, and rinse them often. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:removing-paint-and-plaster-dust}}">removing plaster dust and paint splashes</a> walks through the methods surface by surface.</p>
<p>Larger projects are a different story. Fine dust can damage a household hoover, scraping paint off glass or new worktops takes care, and cleaning a refurbished three-bedroom house properly is several days' work for one person. If you're getting ready to move in, our <a href="{{guide:move-in-cleaning-checklist}}">move-in cleaning checklist</a> is a useful next step, and if the renovation was to add value, our tips on <a href="{{guide:cleaning-before-selling-your-house}}">cleaning before selling your house</a> help the new work show at its best.</p>
<h2>After builders cleaning in Hull and East Yorkshire</h2>
<p>We clean up after extensions, loft conversions, new kitchens and bathrooms, and full refurbishments, from Victorian terraces in <a href="{{area:hull}}">Hull</a> to family homes in <a href="{{area:cottingham}}">Cottingham</a> and <a href="{{area:beverley}}">Beverley</a>. We also clean after shop fit-outs and office refurbishments through our <a href="{{service:commercial-cleaning}}">commercial cleaning</a> service. Our cleaners are insured, bring their own equipment and work to a fixed quote agreed in advance.</p>
<p>See what's covered on our <a href="{{service:after-builders-cleaning}}">after builders cleaning</a> page, or <a href="{{page:contact}}">contact us</a> with a few details and photos of the job and we'll come back with a quote.</p>
