	[
		'slug'        => 'protect-home-during-renovation',
		'title'       => 'How to Protect Your Home from Dust During Renovation',
		'seo_title'   => 'Protect Your Home from Dust During Renovation | Hull',
		'description' => 'How to protect your home from dust during renovation: sealing rooms, covering floors and furniture, managing air and daily habits that cut the clean-up.',
		'category'    => 'after-builders',
		'service'     => 'after-builders-cleaning',
		'related'     => [ 'cleaning-after-home-improvements', 'removing-paint-and-plaster-dust', 'builders-clean-vs-sparkle-clean' ],
		'image'       => 'dust sheets renovation',
		'excerpt'     => 'Practical ways to contain building dust: what to agree with your builder, how to seal rooms, protect floors and furniture, and plan the final clean.',
		'content'     => <<<'HTML'
<p>The best way to protect your home from dust during renovation is to seal off the work area, cover anything that can't be moved, protect the route builders use through the house and control airflow so dust doesn't drift into other rooms. A few hours of preparation before work starts can save days of cleaning afterwards.</p>
<p>Construction dust is far finer than household dust. It slips through gaps under doors, settles inside cupboards and can clog a household hoover within minutes. From a kitchen refit in a Victorian terrace in Hull to an extension on a family home elsewhere in East Yorkshire, the same principles apply.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Agree dust control with your builder before work starts, including who provides screens and floor protection.</li><li>Seal the work area with zip-door dust screens or taped polythene, and block gaps under doors.</li><li>Empty rooms of anything that can be moved, and wrap what can't.</li><li>Some dust will always escape, so plan a professional clean for when the work is completely finished.</li></ul></div>
<h2>Talk to your builder before work starts</h2>
<p>Most good builders put down some protection, but the standard varies. Ask at the quote stage:</p>
<ul>
<li>Will they put up dust screens or zip doors around the work area?</li>
<li>Do their power tools have dust extraction, and do they use a proper site dust extractor rather than a household vacuum?</li>
<li>Can cutting of tiles, brick and timber be done outside?</li>
<li>Will they protect floors along the route from the door to the work area?</li>
<li>How will they tidy up at the end of each day, and what will the site look like when they leave?</li>
</ul>
<p>Cutting brick, concrete, stone and some tiles produces silica dust, which is harmful to breathe. Employers and contractors have legal duties to control hazardous dust, and the Health and Safety Executive's <a href="https://www.hse.gov.uk/coshh/" rel="noopener">COSHH guidance</a> explains the rules. Keep children, pets and anyone with breathing problems well away from cutting work.</p>
<p>It's also worth confirming that the builder's end-of-job clean is a basic tidy, then planning a proper <a href="{{service:after-builders-cleaning}}">after builders clean</a> for when they leave.</p>
<h2>How to seal off the work area</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Fit zip-door dust screens or polythene sheeting</strong> over doorways into the work area, taped to the frame on all sides.</li>
<li><strong>Close internal doors</strong> to other rooms and block the gap at the bottom with a draught excluder or rolled-up old towel.</li>
<li><strong>Tape around loft hatches and cupboard doors</strong> in and near the work area so dust can't settle inside.</li>
<li><strong>Cover extractor fan grilles and cooker hoods</strong> in the work area so they don't pull dust into ducts and filters.</li>
<li><strong>Keep smoke alarms working.</strong> Dust can set them off, and some builders cover the alarm in the work area while they're cutting or sanding. If so, make sure it's uncovered at the end of every working day.</li>
</ol>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Use low-tack decorator's tape on painted walls and door frames. Parcel tape and duct tape can pull paint off when they're removed weeks later.</p></div>
<h2>How to protect floors, carpets and stairs</h2>
<p>Floors take the most punishment, especially the route between the front door and the work area.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Carpets:</strong> self-adhesive carpet protection film is designed for exactly this and stops grit being ground into the pile. Lay it on stairs and landings too.</li>
<li><strong>Hard floors:</strong> use corrugated plastic sheets or hardboard taped at the joints. Grit trapped under a thin dust sheet can scratch wood, laminate and vinyl as people walk over it.</li>
<li><strong>Doormats:</strong> put a sturdy mat inside and outside the entrance the trades use, and shake it out every day.</li>
</ul>
<p>Even with protection, carpets near the work area often need a proper clean at the end. Hot water extraction lifts the fine dust that settles deep in the pile; our guide to <a href="{{guide:carpet-cleaning-methods-explained}}">carpet cleaning methods</a> explains why it outperforms a surface clean, and our <a href="{{service:carpet-upholstery-cleaning}}">carpet and upholstery cleaning</a> team can do it once the builders have gone.</p>
<h2>Clear and cover furniture and belongings</h2>
<ul>
<li>Move everything you can out of the work area and the rooms next to it. Fine dust travels further than you'd expect.</li>
<li>Pack ornaments, books and clothes into lidded plastic boxes rather than open cardboard boxes.</li>
<li>Wrap sofas and mattresses in plastic furniture covers, not just fabric sheets. Plaster dust works into upholstery and is hard to get out.</li>
<li>Take down curtains and blinds where you can, and bag them.</li>
<li>Protect TVs, computers and games consoles, as dust gets into vents and fans.</li>
<li>If the kitchen is near the work, keep food, crockery and utensils in sealed containers or another room.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Manage airflow and ventilation</h2>
<p>Dust goes wherever the air goes, so use that to your advantage:</p>
<ul>
<li>Open a window in the work area so air, and dust, flows out of the house rather than into it.</li>
<li>Keep other rooms' windows closed so you're not drawing a draught through the house from the work area.</li>
<li>Turn off extractor fans in rooms next to the work area, as they can pull dust through gaps under doors.</li>
<li>An air purifier with a HEPA filter in the main living space helps if someone in the house has asthma or allergies. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:bedroom-dust-mite-reduction}}">cleaning for allergies and asthma</a> has more on protecting sensitive households.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Daily habits that keep dust under control</h2>
<ul>
<li>Ask trades to use the same route through the house each day.</li>
<li>Vacuum rather than sweep. Sweeping throws fine dust back into the air. Empty the vacuum and clean its filter often, because plaster dust clogs filters fast and can make the motor overheat.</li>
<li>Damp-wipe surfaces outside the work area every few days with a well-wrung microfibre cloth.</li>
<li>Keep pets out of the work area and wipe their paws, as dust on fur ends up everywhere. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:cleaning-with-pets}}">cleaning a home with pets</a> has more ideas.</li>
<li>Change clothes and shoes after spending time in the work area.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Renovating older homes in Hull and East Yorkshire</h2>
<p>Victorian and Edwardian terraces, like those on the Avenues and off Hessle Road in <a href="{{area:hull}}">Hull</a>, often have lath-and-plaster ceilings and walls, which create a huge amount of dust when they come down. Opening up old chimney breasts can release years of soot too. In these homes, seal the work area even more carefully and keep protection in place until all the old plaster has been bagged and taken away.</p>
<p>Period properties, such as many in <a href="{{area:beverley}}">Beverley</a>, can also hide materials that need specialist handling. Homes built before 2000 may contain asbestos, and older paintwork can contain lead. If you suspect either, stop and get professional advice before anything is sanded, drilled or removed.</p>
<h2>Getting your home back after the builders leave</h2>
<p>However careful everyone is, some dust escapes, and it keeps settling for days after the work stops. Plan the clean-up in this order:</p>
<ol>
<li>The builder removes waste and tools and takes down screens and sheeting.</li>
<li>Snagging and touch-ups are finished.</li>
<li>A professional after builders clean is done, top to bottom.</li>
<li>Carpets and upholstery are cleaned if needed.</li>
<li>Furniture and soft furnishings go back in.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our guide to <a href="{{guide:cleaning-after-home-improvements}}">what's included in an after builders clean</a> explains what to expect, and <a href="{{guide:removing-paint-and-plaster-dust}}">removing plaster dust and paint splashes</a> covers DIY tidying after smaller jobs. If dust has spread through the rest of the house, a <a href="{{service:deep-cleaning}}">deep clean</a> of the untouched rooms brings everything back to normal.</p>
<p>Our team is insured, brings all its own equipment and agrees a fixed quote before starting. Read more about our <a href="{{service:after-builders-cleaning}}">after builders cleaning service</a>, or <a href="{{page:contact}}">get in touch</a> with your expected finish date and we'll pencil in a clean.</p>
HTML,
		'faqs'        => [
			[ 'How do you stop dust spreading during building work?', 'Seal the work area with dust screens or taped polythene, block gaps under doors, protect the route builders use and ventilate the work area to the outside rather than into the rest of the house.' ],
			[ 'Should I stay at home during a renovation?', 'Many people do, but keep children, pets and anyone with asthma away from dusty work, especially cutting brick, concrete or tiles. For major works, staying elsewhere during the dustiest days can help.' ],
			[ 'Is plaster dust harmful?', 'Plaster dust can irritate the eyes, nose, throat and lungs, so wear a suitable dust mask and keep the area ventilated. Dust from cutting brick, concrete and stone can contain silica, which is more harmful.' ],
			[ 'Will dust sheets protect my carpet?', 'They help, but fine dust can pass through fabric sheets and grit underneath can be ground into the pile. Self-adhesive carpet protection film gives better protection.' ],
		],
	],
	[
		'slug'        => 'removing-paint-and-plaster-dust',
		'title'       => 'Removing Plaster Dust and Paint Splashes After Decorating',
		'seo_title'   => 'Removing Plaster Dust and Paint Splashes After Decorating',
		'description' => 'Removing plaster dust and paint splashes after decorating: safe methods for floors, carpets, glass, uPVC and tiles, plus the mistakes that cause damage.',
		'category'    => 'after-builders',
		'service'     => 'after-builders-cleaning',
		'related'     => [ 'cleaning-after-home-improvements', 'protect-home-during-renovation', 'how-to-remove-carpet-stains' ],
		'image'       => 'paint roller wall',
		'excerpt'     => 'Step-by-step methods for clearing plaster dust and removing paint splashes from floors, carpets, glass, uPVC and tiles without damaging new finishes.',
		'content'     => <<<'HTML'
<p>To remove plaster dust after decorating, vacuum first with a filtered vacuum, then wipe surfaces with a damp, well-wrung microfibre cloth, rinsing often and repeating until no white film is left. Fresh paint splashes usually come off with warm soapy water, while dried splashes need softening and careful scraping matched to the surface underneath.</p>
<p>The order and technique matter. Wiping plaster dust with a wet cloth turns it into a paste that smears, and scraping the wrong surface can leave permanent scratches. Our team cleans up after decorators and plasterers in homes across Hull and East Yorkshire, and these are the methods that work.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Always vacuum plaster dust before doing any wet cleaning.</li><li>Use damp, not wet, cloths and change the water often; expect two or three passes.</li><li>Identify the paint type: water-based emulsion lifts with soap and water, oil-based paint needs a suitable solvent.</li><li>Test every method on a hidden area first, especially on carpets, uPVC and new worktops.</li></ul></div>
<h2>Why plaster dust is so hard to clean</h2>
<p>Plaster dust is extremely fine. It hangs in the air for hours after sanding, settles as a film on every horizontal surface and keeps reappearing for days as it drifts down from ledges, light fittings and the tops of doors.</p>
<p>It also reacts badly to the wrong approach. Sweep it and it goes straight back into the air. Wipe it with a wet cloth and it smears into a chalky paste that dries to a white haze. Hoover large amounts with a bagless household vacuum and the fine particles can clog the filter and strain the motor.</p>
<p>That's why a professional <a href="{{service:after-builders-cleaning}}">after builders clean</a> follows a strict order: dry removal first, then damp wiping, then a final dry buff.</p>
<h2>How to remove plaster dust step by step</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Let the dust settle.</strong> Leave it a few hours, ideally overnight, with a window open in the room.</li>
<li><strong>Protect yourself.</strong> Wear a dust mask rated for fine dust, and keep the room ventilated while you work.</li>
<li><strong>Vacuum from top to bottom.</strong> Start with coving, light fittings, the tops of doors and frames, then sills, radiators, skirting boards and finally floors. Use a vacuum with a fine-dust filter, and empty it or change the bag often.</li>
<li><strong>Damp-wipe with microfibre.</strong> Use a bucket of warm water and a cloth wrung out until it's barely damp. Fold the cloth into quarters and turn to a clean side frequently.</li>
<li><strong>Change the water regularly.</strong> Once it looks milky, it's spreading dust rather than removing it.</li>
<li><strong>Dry and buff.</strong> Follow with a dry microfibre cloth to remove the last of the haze.</li>
<li><strong>Repeat a day or two later.</strong> Fine dust keeps settling, so a second pass is almost always needed.</li>
</ol>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Don't forget the hidden places: inside cupboards and drawers, light shades, radiator fins, extractor fan covers and the tops of kitchen wall units. These are where dust lingers and comes back from.</p></div>
<h2>Removing plaster dust from floors, tiles and carpets</h2>
<h3>Hard floors</h3>
<p>Vacuum thoroughly before you mop, then mop with warm water and a little pH-neutral floor cleaner, rinsing the mop frequently. It can take several passes before the white haze disappears. On wood and laminate, keep the mop barely damp and never push a gritty mop head around, as plaster grit scratches.</p>
<h3>Tiles and grout</h3>
<p>Plaster haze on tiles usually comes off with warm water and a microfibre cloth. Hard water marks can look similar, and much of East Yorkshire has hard water; our guide to <a href="{{guide:hard-water-limescale-east-yorkshire}}">removing limescale in hard water areas</a> explains how to tell them apart. For grout haze on newly tiled walls, see our <a href="{{guide:bathroom-deep-clean-guide}}">bathroom deep clean guide</a>.</p>
<h3>Carpets</h3>
<p>Vacuum slowly, in several directions, with the brush bar switched on. Plaster dust sinks deep into the pile and won't come out in one go. Don't wet the carpet while dust is still in it, or you'll create a paste in the fibres. If the carpet still looks grey or white after repeated vacuuming, professional hot water extraction through our <a href="{{service:carpet-upholstery-cleaning}}">carpet and upholstery cleaning</a> service will flush out what's left.</p>
<h2>How to remove paint splashes from different surfaces</h2>
<p>First, work out what kind of paint you're dealing with. Walls and ceilings are usually painted with water-based emulsion. Woodwork paints such as gloss, satin and eggshell can be water-based or oil-based, so check the tin if you still have it.</p>
<h3>Fresh paint splashes</h3>
<p>Deal with them while they're wet. Wipe emulsion and other water-based paint with a damp cloth and a little washing-up liquid. On hard surfaces, wipe oil-based paint with a cloth dampened with white spirit, then wash the area with soapy water.</p>
<h3>Dried paint on glass</h3>
<p>Wet the glass with soapy water and use a glass scraper with a fresh blade held at a low angle, pushing in one direction only. Keep the glass wet throughout. Don't scrape textured, decorative or coated glass; check with the manufacturer if you're unsure.</p>
<h3>Dried paint on uPVC windows and doors</h3>
<p>Avoid metal scrapers and harsh solvents such as acetone or nail varnish remover, which can mark or soften the plastic. Soften the paint with warm soapy water, then ease it off with a plastic scraper or a fingernail. Test any specialist product on a hidden spot first.</p>
<h3>Dried paint on carpets</h3>
<p>For emulsion, soften the spot with warm water and a drop of washing-up liquid, leave it for a few minutes, then gently work the paint out of the fibres with a blunt knife or old toothbrush and blot. Repeat patiently. Oil-based paint on carpet is much harder, and solvents can damage the colour or backing, so it's often best left to a professional. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:how-to-remove-carpet-stains}}">removing common carpet stains</a> covers blotting technique in more detail.</p>
<h3>Wood floors, worktops and tiles</h3>
<p>On sealed wood floors, soften dried paint with warm soapy water and lift it with a plastic scraper. Avoid abrasive pads, which dull the finish. On ceramic tiles, a plastic scraper and soapy water usually shift emulsion. Take extra care on natural stone and new quartz or laminate worktops, and don't use solvents unless the manufacturer says they're safe.</p>
<h2>Mistakes to avoid when cleaning up after decorating</h2>
<ul>
<li>Sweeping plaster dust with a brush.</li>
<li>Using a wet cloth or mop before vacuuming.</li>
<li>Running a household bagless vacuum on heavy plaster dust without emptying it and cleaning the filter regularly.</li>
<li>Scraping paint off dry glass, or off uPVC with a metal blade.</li>
<li>Using acetone, bleach or strong solvents without testing first.</li>
<li>Tipping plaster-laden water down the sink, where it can settle and block pipes. Let it stand, pour the clear water away and bin the sediment.</li>
<li>Putting curtains, rugs and furniture back before the second wave of dust has settled.</li>
</ul>
<h2>When to call in a professional cleaner</h2>
<p>A small decorating job is very manageable yourself. Consider professional help if:</p>
<ul>
<li>A whole room or house has been replastered or sanded.</li>
<li>Paint has dried on delicate surfaces such as natural stone, new worktops, carpets or coated glass.</li>
<li>You don't have the time or equipment for several rounds of dusting and wiping.</li>
<li>You've redecorated a rented home before moving out. Splashes on carpets, sockets and sills can end up on the check-out report, and our <a href="{{service:end-of-tenancy-cleaning}}">end of tenancy cleaning</a> covers that kind of detail.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the work is still under way, our guide to <a href="{{guide:protect-home-during-renovation}}">protecting your home from dust during renovation</a> will cut down the clean-up. For the full picture of what a professional clean covers, read <a href="{{guide:cleaning-after-home-improvements}}">what's included in an after builders clean</a>.</p>
<h2>Cleaning up after decorators in Hull and East Yorkshire</h2>
<p>Our team cleans up after plasterers, decorators and builders in homes and rentals across <a href="{{area:hull}}">Hull</a>, <a href="{{area:pocklington}}">Pocklington</a>, <a href="{{area:driffield}}">Driffield</a> and the rest of East Yorkshire. Our cleaners are insured and vetted, bring their own vacuums, scrapers and products, and work to a fixed quote agreed before they start.</p>
<p>See what's included in our <a href="{{service:after-builders-cleaning}}">after builders cleaning service</a>, or <a href="{{page:contact}}">get in touch</a> with a few photos of the rooms and we'll let you know what's involved.</p>
